At six-thirty, Indiana’s first ballots rolled in;
Joe Weisenthal watched Democratic odds leap toward a win.
The television anchors still cautiously waited;
the markets said a blowout was already indicated.
By eight-thirty, Kalshi showed ninety-eight percent.
The watcher closed the laptop, sleepy and content.

